---

<ChronicleLayout meta={meta}>
  <ArticleHeader
    provenance={meta}
    route={['Spain', 'Andalucía', 'Seville']}
    label="Itinerary · One Day"
    title="One Day in Seville:"
    titleEmphasis="The Route, and the Cost of It"
    standfirst="A single day in Seville is not a planning problem. It is a subtraction problem — and the route below is built around one unexpected move at a quarter past eight in the morning."
    stats={[
      { num: '1', label: 'Day' },
      { num: '5', label: 'Stops' },
      { num: '~4 km', label: 'On Foot' },
      { num: '2', label: 'Tickets to Book' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          One day is not enough for Seville, and every honest account of the city
          says so. But one day is what a great many travellers have — a stop
          between Madrid and the coast, a cruise call at Cádiz, a long layover, a
          Saturday cut out of a working week — and the useful question is not
          whether it is enough. It is which day, out of the several a visitor
          might attempt, actually survives contact with the clock.
        </p>
        <p>
          The answer turns on a fact most itineraries ignore. Seville's two
          essential monuments admit visitors by timed ticket, and the earlier of
          the two does not open until half past nine. That leaves the first hour
          and a quarter of daylight structurally empty — and it happens that the
          city's other great set piece, the one that appears on more postcards
          than anything except the Giralda, is free, unenclosed, unstaffed and
          has no opening hour at all. Plaza de España can be walked at a quarter
          past eight in the morning, when it is close to empty and the low sun
          comes across the tilework at an angle it will not find again all day.
        </p>
        <p>
          Which means a single day in Seville has room for one more major thing
          than it appears to, provided that thing is taken before anything else
          is open. What follows is a route built on that, and on the honest
          accounting that goes with it: five stops, about four kilometres on
          foot, two tickets bought before you arrive, and a plainly named list of
          what the day does not include. The longer versions of this city are
          recorded elsewhere on this site — as
          <a href="/3-days-in-seville/">a three-day route</a> and as
          <a href="/10-days-in-sevilla/">ten days using Seville as a base</a>.
          This is not a compression of either. It is a different argument, because
          at one day the cuts are the content.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/plaza-de-espana-from-the-arcade.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/plaza-de-espana-from-the-arcade-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/plaza-de-espana-from-the-arcade-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/plaza-de-espana-from-the-arcade-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/plaza-de-espana-from-the-arcade-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/plaza-de-espana-from-the-arcade.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Plaza de España in Seville seen from an elevated position on its arcade: the tiled and pinnacled north tower flying a Spanish flag, the long brick and azulejo colonnade, the canal with a rowing boat passing under a balustraded tiled bridge, and several dozen visitors on the paving below."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Half past five in the afternoon, and the plaza is at its most crowded and least kind. Every argument in this article for starting at a quarter past eight is an argument against this photograph."
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="who-for" rule={false}>Who this day is for</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        A first visit, on foot, by a traveller who would rather see four things
        properly than nine things from a doorway. It assumes no car, no tour, no
        transport ticket, an appetite for architecture, and a willingness to stop
        moving between about half past one and three in the afternoon, which is
        not a concession to comfort but the shape the city itself takes. Two
        tickets are booked before arrival. Nothing else needs to be.
      </p>
    </div>

    <AnchorNav
      links={[
        { id: 'before-nine', label: '08:15 · Before the gate opens' },
        { id: 'inside-walls', label: '09:30 · Inside the walls' },
        { id: 'shade-hour', label: '13:30 · The shade hour' },
        { id: 'evening', label: '18:00 · One evening, one choice' },
        { id: 'sunday', label: 'If your day is a Sunday' },
        { id: 'omitted', label: 'What one day cannot hold' },
        { id: 'season', label: 'Season and daylight' },
      ]}
    />
  </div>

  <AtAGlance
    id="glance"
    title="The Day, Hour by Hour"
    meta={[
      {
        label: 'Shape',
        value:
          'A single line about 1.5 km long, from Plaza de España in the south to the Setas in the north. Nothing is walked twice.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Book before you fly',
        value:
          'The Real Alcázar first, then the Cathedral and Giralda. Both are timed. Nothing else on the day needs a reservation.',
      },
      {
        label: 'The one instruction that matters',
        value:
          'Be at Plaza de España by about 08:15. It is the only major sight in Seville that asks nothing of you, and the only hour of the day that is otherwise wasted.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Not on this day',
        value:
          'Triana, Parque de María Luisa, every museum, every private palace, and every day trip. Reasons given at the end.',
      },
    ]}
    daysHeading="The Clock"
    days={[
      { num: '08:15', text: 'Plaza de España, close to empty, with the sun still low across the tiles and the canal.' },
      { num: '09:05', text: 'Walk north to the Alcázar gate — about twelve minutes, through the edge of Parque de María Luisa.' },
      { num: '09:30', text: 'Real Alcázar, first timed slot. Two and a quarter hours, and half of that in the gardens.' },
      { num: '12:00', text: 'Catedral de Sevilla and the Giralda, three minutes away. An hour and a half including the ramps.' },
      { num: '13:30', text: 'Lunch, and then stop. The city does; the route is built to let you.' },
      { num: '15:15', text: 'Archivo General de Indias — free, cool, forty minutes, and closed on Mondays.' },
      { num: '16:00', text: 'Barrio de Santa Cruz, unstructured, as the shadows cross the lanes.' },
      { num: 'Dusk', text: 'Setas de Sevilla, ten minutes north, for the city from above and the Giralda seen from over it.' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <Chapter
      id="before-nine"
      number="I."
      name="Before the Gate Opens"
      epithet="A quarter past eight, and the largest open space in Seville has almost nobody in it"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Plaza de España was built for the Ibero-American Exposition of 1929: a
        brick semicircle nearly two hundred metres across, wrapped around a canal,
        crossed by four tiled bridges, its inner wall broken into alcoves — one for
        each Spanish province, each with a painted map and a tiled bench. It is the
        most theatrical thing in the city and the only major one that is entirely
        free. No gate, no ticket, no attendant, no opening hour.
      </p>
      <p>
        Which is why it belongs at the start of a single day rather than in some
        gap after lunch, for a reason that is physical rather than aesthetic. The
        plaza is unshaded brick and glazed tile from end to end. By noon it is the
        hottest and most crowded ground on this route with nowhere at all to stand
        out of the light; by half past five it looks like the photograph at the top
        of this page. Before about half past nine it is close to empty, cool enough
        to walk slowly, and lit from the side rather than from overhead — the
        difference between seeing tilework and seeing glare.
      </p>
      <p>
        Give it forty-five minutes and walk the whole arc. Then leave: Parque de
        María Luisa begins immediately behind it and is one of the finest urban
        parks in Spain, and today you walk along its edge rather than into it,
        which is the first of the day's small losses. The walk north to the Alcázar
        gate takes about twelve minutes and puts you there at twenty past nine,
        which is exactly where you want to be.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Beat>The one hour of the day nothing else can use, spent on the one thing that asks nothing.</Beat>

    <Chapter
      id="inside-walls"
      number="II."
      name="Inside the Walls"
      epithet="A palace lived in for eight hundred years, and the largest Gothic church on earth, four minutes apart"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The Real Alcázar opens at half past nine, entry is timed, and the early
        slots go first — which means the most important decision of this day is
        made weeks before it, at a keyboard. Take the first slot available. On a
        one-day visit a mid-afternoon Alcázar ticket does not simply move the
        palace later; it dismantles everything after it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Inside, the palace has been continuously inhabited since the twelfth
        century, and what makes it strange is not any single room but the
        decision the Christian kings made when they inherited it: they extended
        what the Moors had built rather than replacing it. Eight hundred years of
        one civilisation finishing another's sentence, in plaster and tile and
        water. The Patio de las Doncellas and the domed Salón de Embajadores are
        the set pieces. The gardens beyond them are close to half the visit and
        are almost always under-budgeted — a walled hectare of clipped
        parterres, tall palms, pavilions and orange trees that most visitors
        glance into on the way out with twenty minutes left.
      </p>
      <p>
        Budget two and a quarter hours and treat the gardens as the second half
        rather than the epilogue. Paths will still be left unwalked. Skip the
        Cuarto Real Alto, which is a separate supplement on a guided visit at set
        times: it is a genuine pleasure and it is the wrong purchase on a day
        this tight.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/alcazar-gardens-from-above.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/alcazar-gardens-from-above-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/alcazar-gardens-from-above-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/alcazar-gardens-from-above-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/alcazar-gardens-from-above.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_PORTRAIT}
      alt="The gardens of the Real Alcázar in Seville seen from above: a grid of clipped box parterres and gravel paths, very tall Canary palms, an ochre garden pavilion among dark trees, a small stone fountain, and a few visitors on the paths."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="Half the Alcázar is outdoors, and it is the half people run out of time for. On one day, the gardens are the reason to buy the earliest ticket rather than the cheapest."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Three or four minutes separate the Alcázar gate from the Cathedral:
        long enough for a coffee, not long enough to plan one. On an ordinary
        weekday, arriving around midday places the visit inside the published
        cultural schedule. Sundays begin later and require the reordered version
        of the day described below. Improvement works on the Giralda's belfry
        have been under way through 2026, the visiting hours have moved because
        of them more than once, and no completion date has been announced. The
        Giralda climb remains open and included in the ticket, although visiting
        hours can change while work continues on the belfry. Check the
        Cathedral's own schedule page in the week before you travel and trust it
        over any itinerary, this one included.
      </p>
      <p>
        The building is the largest Gothic church in the world by volume and it
        does not reward a march between its famous objects. Two things are worth
        the time. The Retablo Mayor is a wall of gilded and polychrome carving
        forty-odd metres high, assembled over most of a century, containing
        something like a thousand figures — an object it is genuinely difficult
        to take in at one standing. And the Giralda is the minaret of the mosque
        the Cathedral replaced, converted rather than demolished, climbed by
        thirty-five shallow ramps rather than stairs, built wide enough — the
        story goes — for a rider to ascend on horseback. There is no lift. The
        ascent and descent take about half an hour and the view is the whole
        argument for making them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/cathedral-retablo-mayor.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/cathedral-retablo-mayor-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/cathedral-retablo-mayor-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/cathedral-retablo-mayor-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/cathedral-retablo-mayor.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_PORTRAIT}
      alt="The Retablo Mayor of Seville Cathedral: a vast gilded altarpiece rising in tiers of carved and painted scenes beneath a coffered gold canopy, with silver candlesticks and an ornate silver altar frontal below."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="The Retablo Mayor took most of a century to assemble and holds something over a thousand carved figures. Fifteen minutes in front of it is a better use of a short day than fifteen minutes finding the tomb of Columbus."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <Chapter
      id="shade-hour"
      number="III."
      name="The Shade Hour, and After"
      epithet="Lunch, an archive that costs nothing, and a quarter of lanes that only works late"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        By half past one the Cathedral is finished and the worst hours of the day
        are beginning. Every plausible one-day itinerary is tempted here, and
        this is where most of them break: they add a museum, or the Torre del
        Oro, or a walk to Triana, and spend the hottest ninety minutes of the day
        crossing open ground. Seville has spent several centuries arriving at a
        different answer, and the route follows it. Eat, properly and slowly, and
        then stop moving for a while.
      </p>
      <p>
        Lunch in Seville is a real meal taken between about two and half past
        three, and on a single day the useful instruction is to eat near where
        you are standing rather than walk somewhere better. The lanes between the
        Cathedral and Plaza de la Alfalfa carry enough good counters to make
        this a low-risk decision; how the ordering actually works, and what the
        words on the board mean, is set out in
        <a href="/seville-tapas-food-guide/">the food guide</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The afternoon then has exactly one indoor stop, and it is free. The
        Archivo General de Indias faces the Cathedral across Plaza del Triunfo
        and holds the paper record of the Spanish empire in the
        Americas — roughly eight kilometres of shelving, uniform boxes, the
        journals of the conquistadors and letters in Columbus's own hand, kept in
        a vaulted Renaissance gallery with a chequered marble floor cut by window
        light. It takes forty minutes, it is air-conditioned, and it is the
        single most under-visited important building in the city. One fact about
        it decides more one-day plans than anything else in Seville:
        <strong>it closes on Mondays.</strong>
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/archivo-de-indias-inscription.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/archivo-de-indias-inscription-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/archivo-de-indias-inscription-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/archivo-de-indias-inscription-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/archivo-de-indias-inscription-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/archivo-de-indias-inscription.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="The words ARCHIVO GENERAL DE INDIAS in gilded raised letters on a pale marble tablet, framed by a dark marble surround and two fluted orange-veined marble columns."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Free entry, forty minutes, air-conditioned, and directly opposite the Cathedral. On a hot afternoon it is the most efficient decision available anywhere on this route."
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Leave Barrio de Santa Cruz until the shadows have crossed the lanes. The
        old Jewish quarter begins a few minutes east of the Cathedral and is the
        part of the day with no ticket, no queue and no fixed route: whitewashed
        walls, orange trees, courtyards behind iron gates, alleys narrow enough
        in places that two people cannot comfortably walk them abreast. At noon
        it is an oven with a crowd in it. At five it is one of the pleasantest
        places in Andalucía to be lost on purpose, and it is the only hour of
        this day with nothing scheduled in it — which, on a day this
        compressed, is not slack. It is the point.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="evening"
      number="IV."
      name="One Evening, One Choice"
      epithet="Two ways to end a single day in Seville, and a recommendation"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        There are two defensible endings and there is not time for both.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The Setas de Sevilla</strong>, ten minutes north of Santa Cruz in
        Plaza de la Encarnación, is an enormous timber lattice carrying a walkway
        across its upper surface. From up there the old town lies out below with
        the Giralda on the skyline — the tower that has been fixing your position
        all day, seen from above for the first time. It stays open long after
        dark, which in high summer is the difference between a viewpoint and a
        furnace, and from April to October a light installation runs across the
        canopy late in the evening.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Triana and Calle Betis</strong>, across the Puente de Isabel II,
        is the other ending: the far bank of the Guadalquivir in low ochre and
        white houses, facing the lit old city across the current. It is
        genuinely the better evening. It is also twenty-five minutes away, on the
        wrong side of a river, in a neighbourhood this day has had no time to
        earn.
      </p>
      <p>
        Take the Setas — not because it is the finer experience but because it
        does something no other stop can. It gives back the ground you have just
        walked, from above, in one view: the plaza in the far south, the palace
        walls, the Cathedral bulk, the lanes you got lost in. It turns four
        monuments into a place with a shape. Triana is the right answer for a
        second day, and there is no second day.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Practical place="Seville — One Day" detail="Five stops · ~4 km on foot · verified 29 July 2026" detailIcon="🚶">
      <PracticalSection label="The route in order" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Plaza de España</strong> — 08:15 to 09:05. Free, unenclosed,
          no ticket, no opening hour. Walk the full arc.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Real Alcázar</strong> — first timed slot, 09:30. Allow 2¼
          hours including the gardens. Open 09:30–19:00 from 1 April to 30
          September and 09:30–17:00 from 1 October to 31 March; closed 1 and 6
          January, Good Friday and 25 December.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Catedral de Sevilla and the Giralda</strong> — 3 minutes'
          walk, from about midday. Allow 1½ hours including the tower ramps.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Lunch and a stop</strong> — 13:30 to about 15:00, near the
          Cathedral rather than somewhere better.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Archivo General de Indias</strong> — 2 minutes' walk. Free, 40
          minutes. Tuesday to Saturday 09:30–17:00, last entry 16:30; Sunday and
          public holidays 10:00–14:00. <strong>Closed Mondays.</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Barrio de Santa Cruz</strong> — 5 minutes' walk, from about
          16:00. Unstructured, and deliberately so.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Setas de Sevilla</strong> — 10 minutes' walk north, timed for
          dusk. General admission from <strong>€16</strong>, including the
          rooftop walkway, a short film and the evening light installation, which
          runs April to October.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="Tickets and booking" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Real Alcázar</strong> — general admission
          <strong>€15.50</strong>; reduced <strong>€8.00</strong> for over-65s,
          students aged 14–30 and European Youth Card holders. Free for
          under-13s with an adult and for visitors with 33% or greater disability
          plus one companion. Buy in advance through
          <a href="https://alcazarsevilla.org/prepara-la-visita/" rel="noopener">the
          Alcázar's own visitor page</a> rather than a reseller. The morning
          slots sell out well ahead; on a one-day visit this is the only booking
          that can genuinely spoil the trip.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Cathedral and Giralda</strong> — <strong>€13.00 online</strong>
          or <strong>€14.00</strong> at the ticket office; reduced
          <strong>€7.00 / €8.00</strong>. Prices current from 1 January 2026.
          Access is timed and capacity limited. Hours, prices and the current
          state of the belfry works are published on
          <a href="https://www.catedraldesevilla.es/en/cultural-visit/schedules-and-rates/" rel="noopener">the
          Cathedral's schedules and rates page</a>.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Nothing else</strong> — Plaza de España, Santa Cruz and the
          Archivo are free and unbooked; the Setas can be bought on arrival.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="Watch-outs" level={3}>
        <div class="tip-row">
          The Cathedral has changed its visiting schedule more than once during
          the Giralda works and says outright that its times may be modified for
          worship and cultural events. Do not build a one-day plan on a schedule
          you read months in advance.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          The Giralda has no lift, and the Alcázar's Cuarto Real Alto is not
          step-free. Santa Cruz is cobbled and uneven throughout. Anyone
          travelling with a mobility constraint should check each venue's own
          accessibility page, which is more current than any itinerary.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          From 1 October the Alcázar closes at 17:00 rather than 19:00. That does
          not affect a 09:30 entry, but it removes the safety margin if your
          morning slips.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          Seville city council has discussed enclosing Plaza de España and
          charging non-residents for entry. Nothing has been implemented and no
          price has been set, but the whole shape of this day rests on the plaza
          being free and open at eight in the morning, so it is worth a check if
          you are reading this well after publication.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>
    </Practical>

    <PullQuote>
      Everything in a one-day itinerary is a decision about what you will not
      see. An itinerary that hides those decisions is not shorter. It is only
      less honest.
    </PullQuote>

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="sunday">If your one day is a Sunday</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Sunday is the constraint worth planning around, because the Cathedral
        does not admit visitors until the afternoon — outside July and August,
        not until half past two. That breaks the middle of the route, and the
        correction is to run the day inside out rather than to give the Cathedral
        up.
      </p>
      <p>
        Keep Plaza de España at a quarter past eight and the Alcázar at half past
        nine; nothing changes there. Then take the <strong>Archivo in the late
        morning instead of the afternoon</strong>, because on Sundays and public
        holidays it opens 10:00 to 14:00 and is otherwise lost, and give the rest
        of the morning to Santa Cruz while the lanes still hold shadow. Eat at
        two. Take the <strong>Cathedral and the Giralda from half past two</strong>,
        which leaves the tower comfortably inside its earlier closing time. The
        Setas still work at dusk. One further Sunday possibility is worth the
        trouble: the Cathedral also releases a limited number of free
        Sunday-afternoon tickets outside public holidays. They require advance
        online reservation; check the official booking calendar for the exact
        available time.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>A Monday</strong> needs a much smaller correction. The Archivo is
        shut, which removes the day's one cool indoor hour; extend lunch, take
        Santa Cruz earlier and slower, and the rest of the route stands. Of the
        five stops, the Archivo is the only one a single day can afford to lose.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="omitted">What one day cannot hold</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Naming the omissions is the honest part of a one-day route, and the list
        is long.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Triana</strong> and the whole west bank — market, ceramics, Calle
        Betis — a morning and an evening in itself, across a river.
        <strong>Parque de María Luisa</strong>, whose edge you walk at nine and
        whose interior you never enter. <strong>The Torre del Oro and the
        Maestranza</strong>, which between them would cost you the Archivo and
        Santa Cruz. <strong>Every museum</strong>, the Museo de Bellas Artes above
        all, which deserves a morning and would take the Alcázar's.
        <strong>Every private palace</strong> — Casa de Pilatos, Condesa de
        Lebrija, Palacio de las Dueñas — any one of which is an hour and a half.
        <strong>The Basílica de la Macarena</strong> and the northern churches.
        And <strong>flamenco</strong>, which needs an evening and cannot share
        one with a rooftop.
      </p>
      <p>
        And most firmly of all, the excursions.
        <a href="/1-day-in-cordoba/">Córdoba is a day in itself</a> and
        <a href="/1-day-in-cadiz/">Cádiz another</a>; both are excellent and
        neither belongs anywhere near a single day in Seville, because each would
        replace your entire day with a train and a different city. The full
        assessment of which excursions are worth a day, and for whom, is
        <a href="/best-day-trips-from-seville/">set out separately</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A specific warning about a specific temptation. The list a first-time
        visitor usually arrives with — Alcázar, Cathedral, Giralda, Santa Cruz,
        Triana, Plaza de España, Parque de María Luisa, the Setas and a
        museum — is not a difficult day. It is an arithmetically impossible one.
        The Alcázar alone is two and a quarter hours, the Cathedral and tower
        another hour and a half, and the walk from the plaza to Triana and back
        across the centre is over five kilometres. Attempting all of it produces
        nine queues, no meal, and a memory of Seville composed mainly of heat and
        pavement. If a longer stay is possible at all, the
        <a href="/3-days-in-seville/">three-day route</a> is where that list
        actually fits.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="season">Season and daylight</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The route is shaped around heat, and only two months change it much. In
        <strong>July and August</strong>, when Seville regularly passes 40°C, the
        quarter-past-eight start stops being clever and becomes compulsory, and
        the afternoon stop lengthens to nothing at all between about half past one
        and half past four; the Setas after dark are the day's mercy. In
        <strong>December and January</strong> the constraint inverts and becomes
        light rather than heat — dusk arrives soon after six, which brings the
        rooftop finish forward into the early evening and makes the whole day
        easier to complete.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything between is straightforward, with two dates worth knowing. From
        <strong>1 October</strong> the Alcázar closes at 17:00 rather than 19:00,
        which does not touch a morning entry but removes your margin if the
        morning slips. And <strong>Semana Santa</strong> and the
        <strong>Feria de Abril</strong> close streets across the centre and move
        opening hours: neither is a week in which a single-day route through the
        old city will survive contact with reality.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="post-closing">
    <div class="narrative prose" style="max-width:820px; margin:0 auto;">
      <p>
        What a single day in Seville can actually deliver is narrower than a list
        of sights and better than it sounds: a palace understood rather than
        toured, a church large enough to change the scale by which you measure
        other churches, an archive nobody else was in, an hour of getting lost on
        purpose, and the city laid out below you at dusk with the tower you have
        been steering by all day standing up out of it. That is four things and a
        view, and it is a real visit.
      </p>
      <p>
        What it cannot deliver is the river, or the same bar twice, or the slow
        business of a place becoming familiar. Those need days rather than hours,
        and they are recorded elsewhere — in
        <a href="/3-days-in-seville/">three days</a>, in
        <a href="/10-days-in-sevilla/">ten</a>, and across
        <a href="/chronicles/">the rest of the archive</a>. A single day is not a
        small version of those. It is a decision to see less, properly, and then
        to come back.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

